INT. COUNTY COURTHOUSE - SUBBASEMENT ARCHIVE - DAY

Steel shelves in ranks, boxed files stacked to the ceiling, a bare bulb on a cord. The air smells of paper rot and lemon oil. At the end of one aisle, a folding table has been set for a deposition.

COUNSEL ELIAS PRESCOTT, 50s, in a rumpled navy suit, sits with a legal pad and a pen he clicks nervously. SALMA RAHIMI-DUBOIS, 30s, a court reporter, hunches over a steno machine, fingers poised. EULALIE FONTAINE, 70s, the witness, in a lace collar and sensible shoes, sits across the table with a handbag in her lap and a face like a closed book.

PRESCOTT
Mrs. Fontaine, please state your name for the record.

EULALIE
Eulalie Marguerite Fontaine.

PRESCOTT
And you were employed as the archivist here from 1988 to 2019?

EULALIE
Thirty-one years.

PRESCOTT
During that time, you maintained the records of all property transfers in this county.

EULALIE
I maintained them. I did not alter them.

PRESCOTT
No one has said you did.

EULALIE
You are about to.

Prescott's pen stops clicking. Salma's fingers flit across the keys, catching every word.

PRESCOTT
Mrs. Fontaine, I'm handing you Exhibit F. Do you recognize it?

EULALIE
It is a deed.

PRESCOTT
For which parcel?

EULALIE
Lot 14, Mill Street.

PRESCOTT
Whose signature is that?

EULALIE
Hmm. It appears to be Mr. Aldous Quinn's.

PRESCOTT
And do you see a notary seal?

EULALIE
I do.

PRESCOTT
Whose?

EULALIE
Mine.

A beat. The bulb sways. Dust floats down through its glow.

PRESCOTT
Did you notarize this document, ma'am?

EULALIE
I did not.

PRESCOTT
But it bears your seal.

EULALIE
It does.

PRESCOTT
Then who stamped it?

EULALIE
Someone with a key to my drawer.

Salma stops typing. Looks up. Eulalie meets her gaze, then turns back to Prescott.

EULALIE (CONT'D)
Mr. Prescott. I have kept that drawer locked for thirty-one years. I have given one key to one person. I have never regretted it until this morning.

PRESCOTT
Who has the key, Mrs. Fontaine?

EULALIE
Your client.

The pen drops. Prescott does not pick it up. His face is white. Salma's fingers resume, steadier now, taking down every syllable.

PRESCOTT
(quietly)
Madam reporter, strike that.

SALMA
I can't strike it. It's on the record.

PRESCOTT
Strike it.

SALMA
(firmly)
No, sir.

Eulalie opens her handbag. Takes out a ring of keys. Sets it gently on the table, with a small clink that echoes through the stacks.

EULALIE
There are two. Mine, and his.

She slides the ring toward Prescott. He stares at it. Overhead, the bulb flickers. The archive hums.
